    "summary": "Kente cloth, a rectangular man’s wrapper composed of 9 hand-woven narrow strips using black and white cotton and rayon. The strips have been machine-sewn together along the selvedges. The strips are designed and arranged to form a chequerboard pattern in the central part of the cloth. Both the ends have a different design which serves as a border to the main field. The warp in all the strips is entirely of black cotton thread. The chequerboard pattern is of rayon, with alternate squares of plain black, or of black with white lozenges. The borders at the ends have five bands of more complicated patterns. The warp ends have been left as a fringe.",
